      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the help. so if i categorize it as an expense, i just need to make sure i have the receipt? also, i saw something online about depreciation for bigger items like furniture... do i have to spread the cost out over a few years or can i just deduct the whole thing at once this year?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, having the receipt is essential for tracking and justifying the expense for tax purposes. For the $5,000 ergonomic chair, since it's considered to have a useful life of more than one year, you are generally required to depreciate the cost over its useful life according to IRS guidelines. To properly account for depreciation in Quickbooks, you'll add the chair as an asset and set its depreciable life. Quickbooks will then work with the IRS-approved methods (e.g., straight-line or declining balance) to depreciate the cost over that period. Consult either a tax professional for precise guidance or refer to IRS regulations regarding the specific useful life of office furniture to ensure proper deduction.",
